    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d383">

                <pb facs="0604" n="502" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg502"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">504.418B1/136½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Chief of the Treaty Division</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Barnes</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Chief of the
                        Division of Latin American Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Wilson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-01-11">January 11,
                        1935</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The treaty for the protection of artistic and scientific institutions and
                    historic monuments was thoroughly considered in the Department last summer.
                    Including, as I recall, the personal views of the Secretary, there was
                    practically no sympathy with it in the Department. The treaty had been before
                    the Department for consideration at least twice before, and there is a large
                    file of correspondence and memoranda of record.</p>
                <p>Secretary of Agriculture Wallace was deeply interested in the treaty and
                    convinced the President that the United States should support it. Last August
                    the President issued a full power in the usual form to Secretary Wallace to sign
                    the treaty on behalf of the United States, and Dr. Rowe<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d383-f1"/> was informed that a full power had been issued to
                    Secretary Wallace. The attitude of the United States toward the signing of the
                    treaty is therefore settled and the next step will be for Secretary Wallace to
                    sign it at the time which may be arranged for by the Governing Board of the Pan
                    American Union. After signature the Pan American Union will, of course, send a
                    certified copy to the Department for transmission to the President and Senate
                    for ratification.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>C[<hi rendition="#small-caps">harles</hi>] M. B[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">arnes</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d383-f1" n="2" xml:id="d383-f1-content">
                <p>Leo S. Rowe, Director General of the Pan American Union.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
